    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Consonants and approximations",
     "texto": [
      "ㄴ and ㅁ are like n and m. ㅅ is usually close to s; before ㅣ it is palatalised, and **시** can be approximated as “shi”, not as an exact equivalent.",
      "ㄱ, ㄷ, ㅂ and ㅈ are lax consonants: at the start they usually sound less aspirated than their partners ㅋ, ㅌ, ㅍ and ㅊ. ㄱ can sound more voiced between vowels; it is not a fixed «g».",
      "ㄹ changes depending on its position: between vowels it sounds like the «d» in «ladder»; at the end of a syllable it is close to «l». ㅎ is aspirated and close to English «h», but it is not exactly the same."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
